d.line("0. **What v12 changes and what is still open (read first).**",
"""0. **What v13 changes and what is still open (read first).** v12 failed two independent reviews (security: 1 blocker, 5 majors, 6 minors; readiness: 1 blocker, 6 majors, 8 minors) and a citation check added three findings (every finding, its fix, its place and the test that fails on the old design: `v13-CHANGES.md`). **Two things the lead must read first. (1) Your answer Q34 (`ORDERS.md:216-217`) says the database rule `usd >= 0` "only refuses a negative total". That premise is wrong for the way our store writes a spend change today (security finding B1; run on Postgres 16.15 here, `evidence-handoff-v13/raw-pg-b1-v13.txt` and `raw-pg-real-code-v13.txt`).** `add_spend` writes a change as an INSERT with a conflict rule (`store/postgres.py:416-431`), and Postgres tests the rule on the row the INSERT proposes, before it looks at the conflict. So once the rule is applied, every give-back and every settle that lowers a total is refused (error 23514), even when the stored total would stay above zero; every call then keeps its whole worst case and an agent reaches its daily limit within a few calls. The fix is in the store code (S-2a): a negative change is written as an UPDATE of the existing row, a positive change stays an INSERT with a conflict rule (5.4 item 4); DB-1 must not be applied before that code runs (start gate G-4). **(2) File 2 of v12 (the decrease record) is not part of Q34.** v13 takes it out of DB-1 and makes it its own PR, DB-2, which needs its own yes from you (question L9). **Still open after v13, each stated where it lives:** (a) a 429 or 5xx storm can still use up an agent's day, because those failed attempts stay booked (5.3 outcome table, 5.9 row 8; releasing 429 needs the owner's yes, question O1); (b) the runtime database role can still lower any spend total and switch the kill switch off: neither DB-1 nor DB-2 closes that (5.6 item 6; the real fix is L-2, which needs your yes); many small lowerings stay possible and are only recorded, not stopped; (c) the price unit of the guardrail vendor and the error shapes of the vendor kinds are vendor facts I could not check (5.7, 5.9); (d) what was and was not run: item 2.""")

d.line("1. **Migration `0006`",
"""1. **Migration `0006` (the database rule "a spend total may not be negative") is settled by the lead's answer Q34 (`ORDERS.md` line 217): no veto, keep it, it ships with its own negative test and is applied only on an owner yes. v13 keeps that and adds one condition that Q34 did not know about: the store code of S-2a must be merged and running first (item 0, point 1).** It is the held file of PR **DB-1** (merge order 4, section 7.1). Two things the lead should read: (a) the rule has one clause beyond Q34, `usd < 'Infinity'`, explained in section 5.6 item 1 (run on Postgres 16.15: it refuses a not-a-number total and an infinite one; a plain `usd >= 0` lets both in); (b) the owner yes is made checkable, not only promised: the file waits in a folder the migration runner does not read, and the runner refuses it if it is moved without a written owner yes (5.6 item 1). **File 2 of v12 (a table, a function and triggers that record every lowering of a total) is now PR DB-2, held the same way, and is NOT presented as part of Q34:** it needs its own lead yes (question L9). S-1, S-2a and S-2b need nothing from DB-1 or DB-2; until they are applied the exposure of section 5.6 item 6 stays.""")

